class:: Tempo
summary:: Causal tempo and confidence
categories:: UGens>Analysis>Rhythm
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/Onsets2, Classes/BeatTrack3

description::
Autocorrelation times Fourier support ranks BPM candidates, with a log-tempo continuity prior and explicit half/double-tempo resistance. A causal clock smooths the selected tempo; confidence is heuristic evidence, not a calibrated probability.

The periodicity-analysis source is Grosche, Müller and Kurth (2010), link::https://resources.mpi-inf.mpg.de/MIR/tempogramtoolbox/2010_GroscheMuellerKurth_TempogramCyclic_ICASSP.pdf::. MIR2 uses trailing absolute-BPM windows and an original causal tracker; it does not implement cyclic tempo folding or claim equivalence with the paper. Output begins after the requested warmup and sufficient periods, and is held between analyses. Silence clears evidence as it leaves the trailing window. Tempo ambiguities, syncopation, sparse events and changing meter remain limitations. Vanilla BeatTrack also estimates pulse timing; MIR2 exposes the evidence distribution, alternative tempi and explicit continuity controls.

classmethods::
method:: kr
argument:: in
Nonnegative control-rate onset strength, such as Onsets2 novelty. Audio-rate input is rejected.

argument:: minBPM
Lowest tempo in BPM, at least 20; literal at construction.

argument:: maxBPM
Highest tempo in BPM, greater than minBPM and at most 600; literal at construction.

argument:: windowTime
Trailing history in seconds. Rhythm analysis accepts 0.5–16, default 6; onset/tempo statistics accept 0.05–60, default 4.

argument:: hopTime
Analysis interval in seconds, 0.02–2, default 0.1; rounded to a whole number of pooled feature frames.

argument:: priorBPM
Initial preferred tempo, default 120 BPM. This is a weak initial prior, not a forced tempo.

argument:: continuity
Log-tempo continuity weight, 0–20, default 3. Larger values resist changes, including half/double-tempo switches.

argument:: tempoSmooth
Tempo smoothing time in seconds, default 0.4; zero follows each new estimate.

argument:: warmup
Minimum accumulated history in seconds, default 2 for tempo tracking. Each candidate additionally needs two complete periods.

argument:: featureRate
Requested onset-envelope rate, 20–200 Hz, default 100. Maximum pooling spans a rounded number of server control blocks; the actual rate is used in all timing calculations.

argument:: numBins
Literal number of linearly spaced BPM bins, 8–512. Defaults to 128 for vectors and 271 for scalar estimates.

returns::
[BPM, confidence]; BPM is zero without sufficient evidence.

examples::
Tempo extracts causal tempo in BPM and continuous confidence from onset novelty using log-continuity priors and octave-jump resistance. In this example, it acts as an automatic master clock: detected tempo synchronizes an algorithmic arpeggiator that plays sixteenth-note synth sequences in lockstep with acoustic drums. An interactive code::MIR2GUI.rhythm:: displays the real-time tempo dial and confidence meter.
code::
(
Routine({
    var path = MIR2GUI.soundPath("drums.wav");
    ~mir2Buffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~mir2Bus = Bus.control(s, 2);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Example = {
        var source = PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1);
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(1024), source, 0.25);
        var onset = Onsets2.kr(chain, threshold: 0.15, fftSize: 1024, hop: 0.25);
        var tempo = Tempo.kr(onset[1], minBPM: 60, maxBPM: 200, priorBPM: 120, tempoSmooth: 0.2);
        var bpm = tempo[0].clip(40, 240);
        var conf = tempo[1];

        // Synced synth arpeggio running at quarter-note rate
        var clock = Impulse.kr((bpm / 60.0).max(0.2));
        var seq = Demand.kr(clock, 0, Dseq([60, 63, 67, 70, 72, 70, 67, 63].midicps, inf));
        var synthArp = SinOsc.ar(seq, 0, Decay2.kr(clock, 0.005, 0.1) * conf.lag(0.05) * 0.15);
        var sound = (source * 0.25) + synthArp;

        if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { Out.kr(~mir2Bus, [bpm, conf]) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2) ! 2
    }.play;

    ~mir2GUI = MIR2GUI.rhythm("Tempo Dial", ~mir2Bus);
}).play(AppClock);
)
::
code::
(
~mir2Example.free;
if(~mir2GUI.notNil) { ~mir2GUI.close };
if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { ~mir2Bus.free };
if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) { ~mir2Buffer.free };
)
::

